A root component's state, mounted on two windows, on every target that renders
them. This is the fixture that pins the per-platform difference, because the
difference is the documented behaviour rather than an accident.

`main()` at the root of the file renders the component, and the inliner
substitutes its body into the package body -- so the one `hits__inst0` the
splice named is the var both windows read, and what *that* means is the
platform's answer. A target whose windows are one
process shares one cell: bubbletea, fyne and gtk4 each put `hits` in one Model,
and the click in window A is read by window B. A target whose windows are
separate documents copies it: html writes its own `var state = { hits: 0 }`
into index.html and into b.html, because that is what two pages are. Neither is
imposed by the lowering -- forcing either would be the language overriding the
platform it compiled to -- and the author picks a target knowing it. Top-level
state shared across windows is a performance tool, not the default way to write
an application.

The sharp edge is the shared pointer reaching one Model through two owners. It
must emit one field: CodegenCtx.ModelState dedupes by the *ir.Var, and without
that the Go targets declared `hits int` twice in the same struct and did not
compile.

`bump` reaches no target at all, and that is the right answer rather than a
gap: it is pure, `passInlinePure` folds its one call, and the shake then has a
declaration nothing names. It used to be emitted because `ir.Window.Funcs` was
a shake root, so the hoist kept alive a func the program had already finished
with. root_component_mutating_func.txtar is where a root component's func
survives to every backend.

`__inst0` throughout is that splice, and it is the whole of what has replaced
the lift: nothing scans the declarations for the root family any more, so a
root component nobody instantiates renders nothing and declares nothing.

Reading a var owned by several windows is what CodegenCtx.sharedWindowScope is
for. EntryWindow declines to say which window a multi-window program's single
Model is scoped to, and it is right to -- two windows' `count` are two names --
but a declaration owned by *all* of them is one *ir.Var reachable from each, so
scoping to it chooses nothing. Without that, every read here rendered as a bare
`hits` the emitted Go never declared, standing beside the Model field it should
have projected onto.

Two pre-existing multi-window limits show through and are not this fixture's
subject. android renders the first window and drops the second, which is why
its golden below is one screen. And gtk4 builds only the entry window's tree
while emitting updaters for every window's nodes, so it names a handle it never
declares -- that reproduces with package-level state and two file-root windows,
so gtk4 is left out of the output block here rather than having the defect
baked into a golden.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `(?sm)^\thits\s+int$.*^\thits\s+int$` -- one Model, one cell: the var is mounted on both windows and must be declared once
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `(?sm)^\thits\s+int$.*^\thits\s+int$` -- as above, for fyne's Model
deny * `(?i)\bbump\b` -- a pure func whose only call was folded reaches no backend; the hoist used to keep it alive as a shake root
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `[(,+]hits\b` -- a read of the shared var is `m.hits`; the bare `Sprint(hits)` is the scope gap this fixture is for
deny out/none/html/b.html `var state = \{\s*\}` -- the second page carries the state too; an empty state object is the defect this fixture is for
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main root {
    var hits = 0

    func bump(n int) => n + 1

    ui.window #a(title="A", href="/") {
        ui.text(value="a {hits}")
        ui.button(text="go", @click { hits = bump(hits) })
    }

    ui.window #b(title="B", href="/b.html") {
        ui.text(value="b {hits}")
    }
}

main()
